00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:07.510 align:start position:0%
european union ministers have reached  agreement on new rules governing the way  information about agricultural products  is promoted on europe's internal market  as well as to the outside world  the rules would better inform consumers  and stakeholders  about the quality of food produced in  europe to help boost competitiveness and  find new markets  we now have a mandate to negotiate  with the european parliament i dare  to say that this file is a close to  conclusion  and we hope soon to be able to adopt  a proposal at first reading before  the end of the term of office of the  current parliament  the ministers also heard a report from  the european commission on the  possibility of broadening rules  on mandatory origin labeling for meat  views differ among the member states on  the issue many favor mandatory labeling  for meat used as an ingredient  with some wanting the specific country  of origin marked  others prefer labeling to be done on a  voluntary basis  the report weighs the need for consumer  information against the costs  and feasibility of introducing such a  system  the commission also presented a proposal  for new rules on organic production and  farming  and the labeling of organic products the  aims to simplify legislation and cut red  tape  as well as tighten controls on the  industry  fisheries ministers meanwhile discussed  a new five-year arrangement for managing  stocks of mackerel  in the north east atlantic the coastal  state's mackerel arrangement involves  the eu  norway and the faroe islands and allows  joint management of the stock so that  fishing remains sustainable  the ministers expressed regret that  iceland was not taking part
 

